# Zero-width / invisible Unicode characters in source.
#
# Why this exists: zero-width and invisible code points (U+200B zero-width space,
# U+200C/200D joiners, U+FEFF BOM/zero-width no-break space, U+2060 word joiner)
# are not visible to a reviewer but are present in the bytes. They can smuggle
# meaning past a code review, break naive tokenisers, or watermark/fingerprint
# text. They almost never belong inside program source.
#
# What it flags: a line containing any of those invisible code points, matched as
# the LITERAL UTF-8 bytes. A leading U+FEFF BOM on the very first line of a file
# is a common, benign case; this fires on any occurrence, so scope with
# exclude_paths or suppress inline (keur:ignore SEC-ZWSP-001) where a BOM is
# intentional.
#
# WARNING, not block: usually a smell or a copy-paste artefact, occasionally
# legitimate (BOM). Worth a human look, not an automatic fail.
id: SEC-ZWSP-001
title: Zero-width / invisible Unicode characters in source
category: security
severity: warning
engine: pattern
action: warn
patterns:
  - regex: [​‌‍﻿⁠]
    message: "Zero-width/invisible Unicode character (U+200B/200C/200D/FEFF/2060) — invisible to a reviewer but present in the bytes; can hide meaning or watermark text. Remove unless it is an intentional BOM. @see SEC-ZWSP-001"
confidence: 0.6
fix: "Delete the invisible character. If it is a byte-order mark (U+FEFF) that must stay, keep it only as the first byte of the file and suppress with 'keur:ignore SEC-ZWSP-001' on that line."
